        "say": "Alright, this is Property and Casualty — the coverage that protects your home and your car, and it starts with how a homeowners policy is actually laid out. Every HO policy splits into two halves, and the exam wants you to know which coverages live where. Section One is the property side — your stuff. Coverage A is the dwelling itself, the structure plus anything attached to it. Coverage B is other structures, the detached ones like a garage or a fence, and it runs about ten percent of Coverage A. Coverage C is personal property, your contents, and that's roughly fifty to seventy percent of Coverage A. Coverage D is loss of use, the additional living expenses if you can't live in the home while it's repaired. Then Section Two is the liability side — your responsibility to other people. Coverage E is comprehensive personal liability, bodily injury and property damage you cause to others. Coverage F is medical payments to others — and don't let the short label fool you, because it's broader than just paying a guest's doctor bill. Coverage F actually covers three things: the medical payments themselves, the claim expenses tied to those payments, and damage to other people's property. So it's medical payments, claim expenses, and property damage to others, all bundled under that one Coverage F line. Here's the key rule they test: Coverages B, C, and D are all priced as percentages of the Coverage A limit. So the insured really only chooses one number — the Coverage A dwelling limit — and everything else scales off of it. Memory hook: just march down the alphabet, A dwelling, B other structures, C contents, D loss of use, and you've got Section One cold."
