        "say": "Now the fine print — the standard provisions, and several are exam staples. The grace period is typically thirty days after a missed premium before the policy lapses. Reinstatement revives a lapsed policy, usually within three to five years, but you will need evidence of insurability plus back premiums with interest. The free look lets you cancel a brand-new policy within ten to thirty days for a full refund. Incontestability is big: after two years, the insurer cannot deny a claim for a misrepresentation — fraud is the only exception. The suicide clause runs the same two years — death by suicide inside that window returns only the premiums, no death benefit. Misstatement of age does not void anything; it simply adjusts the benefit to what the premium actually paid would have bought at the true age. And the spendthrift clause stops a beneficiary from pledging or assigning proceeds before they are paid, shielding them from the beneficiary's creditors. Now the nonforfeiture options, which is where students blank — these are what a permanent policy's cash value can become if you stop paying. Cash surrender: take the money and the policy ends. Reduced paid-up: use the cash value to buy a smaller amount of the same permanent insurance, fully paid, no more premiums, lasting for life. Extended term: use the cash value to buy term at the same face amount for as long as it will last — and this is the default if you choose nothing. So reduced paid-up shrinks the face but keeps it forever; extended term keeps the face but shortens the time. For settlement, the proceeds can be paid as interest only, a fixed period, a fixed amount, or life income annuitized over the beneficiary's life. And bank the beneficiary rules — there are two separate axes here and the exam tests both. First, primary versus contingent: the primary beneficiary is first in line, and the contingent, or secondary, beneficiary collects only if all the primaries predecease the insured. Second, revocable versus irrevocable: most designations are revocable, meaning the owner can change them at any time, while an irrevocable beneficiary cannot be removed and must consent to policy changes like loans, surrender, or assignment."
