        "say": "Last piece — when does an annuity actually fit, and how is it protected. It is suitable for long-term retirement income and longevity protection, for downside market protection through fixed or indexed products, for someone who has already maxed out qualified retirement contributions, and for a low-risk-tolerance client in the decumulation phase. It is not suitable for short-term needs, because surrender charges bite; not for someone chasing high uncapped growth; not as an emergency fund, because of that LIFO taxation; and not for a client who simply lacks savings and needs maximum accumulation. Against a traditional I-R-A, the trade-off is clean: the I-R-A wins on lower fees, uncapped growth, liquidity, and deductible contributions, while the annuity wins on downside protection, longevity protection, no required distributions on nonqualified money, and no contribution limits — both give you tax-deferred growth. On protection, remember annuities and life insurance are backed by state guaranty associations if the insurer goes into receivership, with limits that vary by state, typically one hundred thousand to five hundred thousand dollars — and crucially, that is not federal backing the way F-D-I-C coverage is. Behind the suitability rule sits a real standard: most states have adopted the NAIC Model number 275, which layers a best-interest standard on top with four duties — Care, Disclosure, Conflict of interest, and Documentation. And note the stacking: a variable annuity recommendation is also a securities recommendation, so it picks up FINRA and Regulation Best Interest on top of the state rule, while a fixed nonqualified annuity is state-only — and a CFP professional is held higher still, to the Board's fiduciary duty. So that is Annuities end to end: the classification grid, the product types, the guaranteed-living-benefit alphabet, the payout choices, the exclusion-ratio taxation, and where these actually belong in a plan. Match the product to the goal — longevity income — and these questions become points."
