   "at": "duration vs. convexity",
   "say": "Look at this picture of a twenty year, six percent bond. The blue curve is the bond's actual price as yields move, and the dashed red line is what duration predicts. Duration is a straight line touching the curve at today's yield. Because the real curve bows above that line, duration overstates how much the price falls when yields rise and understates how much it rises when yields fall. That bow is convexity, and it's good for the bond holder. The exam tests the idea, not a convexity calculation. To a client: when rates move a lot, your bond's price moves a bit more kindly than the simple estimate suggests."
